+---
+id: task-001-implement-json-pointer-resolution-rfc-6901
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — Implement JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document. Implement JSON Pointer,
+as specified in RFC 6901. This is the first of several features that build on each other; the next
+ones will use it.
+
+Export a function `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. Given a JSON document and a
+JSON Pointer string, it returns the value the pointer refers to. It throws an error when the pointer is
+not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it does not resolve to a value of the document.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `""` (empty pointer) refers to the whole document.
+2. A non-empty pointer must start with `/`; each `/`-separated reference token selects one member.
+3. Escapes: `~1` decodes to `/` and `~0` decodes to `~`. Any other `~` sequence is an invalid pointer.
+4. Objects: a token selects the own member with that name. Inherited properties (for example
+   `constructor`, `__proto__`) do not resolve.
+5. Arrays: a token must be an array index, `0` or a digit string without leading zeros. `-` never
+   resolves. An index past the end does not resolve.
+6. Descending into a primitive value (or `null`) does not resolve.
+7. A pointer that does not resolve throws an error. A pointer that is not a valid JSON Pointer throws
+   an error (for example a non-empty pointer without a leading `/`, or a bad `~` escape).
+8. A value that is `null` or `false` or `0` is a value: it is returned, not treated as missing.
+9. Input is typed explicitly; the implementation has no runtime dependency.
+
+## Test classification
+
+- Happy path: characterization is not applicable (new behaviour), so these tests are red-first.
+- Edge and error paths: each criterion 3 to 8 has at least one test.
